    full_text: `Along the sacred stone ghats of Varanasi more than twenty-five centuries ago, a gentle teacher sat in an open-air medical school surrounded by eager students. His name was Sushruta. In an era when sickness was often attributed to angry spirits and surgery was feared as a bloody butchery, Sushruta elevated surgical medicine into a compassionate, noble science.\n\nOne evening, a desperate traveler arrived at Sushruta's door. The man was weeping, covering his mutilated face with a ragged cloth. In ancient warfare and punishment, chopping off an adversary's nose was a cruel, common humiliation, leaving victims permanently disfigured and cast out of society. The traveler begged for mercy: \"Master healer, can you restore my face so I may look upon my children without shame?\"\n\nSushruta did not flinch. He comforted the stranger with herbal infusions and prepared his surgical pavilion. Long before sterile modern operating theaters, Sushruta emphasized absolute cleanliness: scrubbing hands, fumigating the chamber with antiseptic herbal incense, and heating surgical blades over hot coals to sterilize them.\n\nTo recreate the missing nose, Sushruta devised a stroke of surgical genius known today as the \"Indian forehead flap\" rhinoplasty. First, he measured the shape of the missing nose using a clean leaf. Then, with a razor-sharp leaf-shaped scalpel, he carefully traced the pattern onto the traveler's forehead, dissecting a flap of living skin while leaving a narrow pedicle intact at the base to maintain blood flow!\n\nRotating the living flap downward, he skillfully sculpted it over the nasal cavity, inserting two hollow castor-oil plant reeds into the nostrils so the patient could breathe freely. He dusted the delicate wound with healing powders of red sandalwood and licorice, bandaging it with clean cotton cloth.\n\nWeeks later, when the dressings were gently unwrapped, a miracle greeted the patient's mirror: the living skin flap had healed seamlessly into a natural, handsome nose! The man wept tears of joy—his dignity and life were restored.\n\nSushruta recorded over three hundred surgical procedures in his monumental compendium, the *Sushruta Samhita*. He described one hundred and twenty-one surgical instruments—forceps shaped like lion jaws and eagle beaks, scalpels, trocars, and fine bone saws. He performed cataract bone-needle couching, cesarean deliveries, and bladder stone extractions.\n\nSushruta insisted that a surgeon must practice endlessly before touching living human flesh. He had his students practice delicate incisions on the rind of watermelons, practice probing on hollow bamboo, and perform bandaging on stuffed life-sized cloth mannequins. \"A physician who knows only book learning,\" Sushruta taught, \"is like a soldier with a shield but no sword; only patient practice with compassionate intent makes a true healer.\" Today, Sushruta is revered worldwide as the founding father of surgery.`,
