    full_text: `In the hot summer of 1609, news swept through the canals of Venice like wildfire: a Dutch spectacle-maker named Hans Lippershey had constructed a curious tube with two glass lenses that made distant church steeples appear nearby. While merchants saw it as a military gadget or carnival novelty, a forty-five-year-old mathematics professor in Padua named Galileo Galilei realized its true destiny.\n\nWithout ever having seen the Dutch spyglass, Galileo applied his profound knowledge of optical refraction. Working late into the night over his grinding lathe, he polished fine discs of Venetian glass—combining a plano-convex objective lens with a plano-concave eyepiece into a long lead tube. While the Dutch toy magnified only three times, Galileo’s third telescope magnified objects thirty times and made them a thousand times brighter!\n\nOn a crisp, crystal-clear winter evening—January 7, 1610—Galileo carried his telescope to the roof of his house in Padua and aimed it at Jupiter, the brightest wandering star in the sky.\n\nLooking through the brass eyepiece, Galileo gasped. Flanking the glowing disk of Jupiter were three tiny, sparkling pinpricks of light—two to the east and one to the west—lined up in an exquisitely straight row. At first, Galileo assumed they were ordinary faint background stars.\n\nBut the next night, January 8, he returned to his telescope. To his utter bewilderment, all three stars had shifted! They were now all clustered to the *west* of Jupiter! Over the next two weeks, Galileo watched in breathless fascination as a fourth tiny star appeared. Night after night, the four little stars danced back and forth, disappearing behind Jupiter's bulk and reappearing on the other side.\n\nIn a flash of revolutionary understanding, Galileo solved the mystery: they were not distant stars at all! They were four magnificent moons orbiting around Jupiter, carried along with the giant planet just as our Moon is carried along by the Earth!\n\nThis single observation dealt a fatal, devastating blow to traditional Aristotle-Ptolemaic cosmology. For centuries, conservative university professors insisted that the Earth MUST be the motionless center of the cosmos because if the Earth moved, it would leave its solitary Moon behind in space. Now, before Galileo's eyes, was undeniable physical proof: Jupiter was moving through space, and it carried four moons along with it!\n\nGalileo rushed his findings to the printers in Venice, publishing *Sidereus Nuncius* (*The Starry Messenger*) in March 1610. The booklet sold out in days. Galileo showed the world that the Moon was not a smooth, divine crystal sphere, but rough and pockmarked with rugged craters and mountain ranges; and that the Milky Way was not a celestial cloud, but millions of distant, teeming stars.\n\nBy turning a hand-ground spyglass to the heavens, Galileo shattered two millennia of philosophical dogma. He proved that truth is not discovered in dusty ancient books, but through the sharp glass of honest, empirical observation.`,
