    full_text: `In the imperial city of Prague in the early 1600s, inside a dusty room cluttered with astronomical star tables, sat an impoverished, sickly mathematical genius named Johannes Kepler. Kepler had poor eyesight and crippled hands from childhood smallpox, but within his brilliant mind burned a mystical devotion to uncovering the mathematical harmony of God’s universe.\n\nUpon the sudden death of the flamboyant Danish nobleman Tycho Brahe in 1601, Kepler inherited Tycho’s treasure: thirty years of the most painstakingly accurate, naked-eye observational data of planetary positions ever recorded. In particular, Tycho had assigned Kepler the toughest riddle of all: the orbit of Mars.\n\nFor more than two thousand years, starting with Plato and Aristotle, astronomers held one dogma as sacred and untouchable: because the heavens were divine, all celestial bodies MUST move in \"perfect circles\" at unvarying, constant speeds.\n\nKepler tried with heroic determination to fit Tycho’s Mars observations into circular orbits. He calculated, erased, and recalculated across hundreds of enormous folio sheets. Finally, he constructed a circular model that matched Tycho's observations almost perfectly—except for one tiny, maddening error: a discrepancy of just eight arcminutes (barely one-fourth the width of the full Moon)!\n\nOther astronomers urged Kepler to brush the eight arcminutes aside as an observational fluke or round it off. But Kepler refused to lie to nature. \"If I had believed that we could ignore these eight arcminutes,\" Kepler wrote, \"I would have patched up my hypothesis. But since it was not permissible to ignore them, those eight arcminutes alone pointed the way to a complete revolution in astronomy!\"\n\nHe threw away years of grueling labor and started over from scratch. He tested ovals, egg shapes, and mathematical curves. Finally, in an agonizing flash of insight in 1605, Kepler tried an ellipse—an elongated oval with two focal points, like a circle stretched gently between two pins.\n\nTo his overwhelming joy, every single one of Tycho’s observations fell into place with divine, absolute perfection!\n\nThe Sun was not at the dead center, but at one focus of the ellipse. Furthermore, as Mars drew closer to the blazing Sun (perihelion), it surged forward faster, and as it swung into the cold outer depths (aphelion), it slowed down—sweeping out equal areas of space in equal intervals of time!\n\nIn 1609, Kepler published his First and Second Laws of Planetary Motion in his masterpiece, *Astronomia Nova* (*The New Astronomy*). A decade later, in *Harmonices Mundi* (*The Harmony of the World*, 1619), he unveiled his Third Law, linking a planet's orbital period to its distance from the Sun ($P^2 = a^3$).\n\nKepler proved that the universe was not a fragile clockwork of divine circles, but a dynamic, breathing clock driven by physical forces. By refusing to compromise on eight tiny arcminutes of honest evidence, Johannes Kepler unlocked the true geometric architecture of the solar system.`,
